  "conclusion": "HYPOTHESIS REJECTED. Night session return has NEGATIVE correlation with same-day day session (r=-0.0824, direction 48.4%), meaning night session information is already priced in by day open, and market slightly mean-reverts. Lagged signal (t-1 night → t day) shows weak positive correlation (r=+0.1399, direction 51.2%, Sharpe=0.35) but is NOT statistically significant (DM |t|=0.90 < 3.0). No strategy beats buy-and-hold after transaction costs. Key insight: night session prices are SAME instrument (TX futures), so there is no real information transmission — the overnight gap already incorporates the night session move. This is fundamentally different from cross-market lead-lag (US→TW stocks).",
  "limitations": [
    "TX cost assumption 1bp may be optimistic for retail (spread + slippage)",
    "Near-month rollover not modeled (using TX1 files directly)",
    "Night session liquidity varies (thin ~01:00-04:00)",
    "No margin/leverage modeling",
    "Signal is same-calendar-day (night ends 05:00, day starts 08:45)"
  ]
